<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glucosinolate activation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5267" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Arabidopsis thaliana col, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: Glucosinolates &quot;Glucosinolates&quot;| are substituted &amp;beta;-thioglucoside &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-hydroxysulfates, formed by certain planst from any one of eight amino acids, namely, alanine, valine, leucine, isoleucine, phenylalanine, methionine, tyrosine and tryptophan. Over 115 naturally occurring glucosinolates have been identified |CITS: [18458837]|. Much of the diversity amongst glucosinolates arises from the addition of different sized alkyl groups to the side chain of the amino acids, principally valine, phenylalanine and methionine. Glucosinolates are found prominently in the order |FRAME: TAX-3700|, which includes cabbage, mustard, oilseed rape, broccoli, and the model plant Arabidopsis, and are responsible for the typical sharp taste and odor of these plants. The glucosinolates are hydrolyzed by the plant to compounds that are toxic to herbivores, and play an important role in plant defense. The hydrolysis of is catalyzed by |FRAME: AT5G26000-MONOMER &quot;myrosinases&quot;| (|FRAME: EC-3.2.1.147|), enzymes that are physically segregated from glucosinolates within the intact plant in specialized myrosin cells, and are brought into contact by tissue damage |CITS: [Bones96]|. The products of the myrosinase reaction are unstable |FRAME: THIOHYDROXIMATE-O-SULFATES &quot;thiohydroximate-O-sulfates&quot;| that decompose to different types of products, including nitriles, isothiocyanates, thiocyanates, oxazolidine-2-thiones, and epithionitriles. The type of product(s) that form depends on the the chemical nature of the side chain of the parent glucosinolate and the presence of different enzymes. Folowing the hydrolysis of the glucose moiety, glucosinolates with aliphatic side chains generally decompose spontaneously to yield isothiocyanates at a neutral pH, and in the absence of other enzymes (as in some Capparales species), isothiocynates are the only breakdown products |CITS: [11752388]|. However, acidic pH or the presence of |FRAME:FE+2| ions favors the production of nitriles |CITS: [Gil80]|. In some plants additional proteins are involved in glucosinolate hydrolysis. The presence of |FRAME: EC-4.8.1.5| (NSP) results in conversion of thiohydroximate-O-sulfates to bioactive nitriles. The presence of |FRAME: EC-4.8.1.6| (ESP) results in conversion to either nitriles or epithionitriles, depending on the substrate |CITS: [11752388]|. If the parent glucosinolate possessed a terminal alkene group, epithionitriles are formed (by transfer of the sulfur atom from the basic glucosinolate backbone to the terminal alkene residue of the side chain), whereas other glucosinolates are converted to simple nitriles |CITS: [Daxenbichler68][4130022][MacLeod85][11752388][15845404]|. In one documented case the presence of |FRAME: EC-4.8.1.7| (TFP) results in formation of thiocyanates. Conversion of glucosinolates to isothiocyanates has also been observed in bacterial members of the gut microbiota |CITS: [34444909]|. The first isolation of bacterial myrosinase was made already in 1974 |CITS: [Tani74]|. In 2016 two more bacterial myrosinases were isolated, one of which was reported to belong to the glycohydrolase 3 (GH3) family |CITS: [26820976][El-Shora16]|. Several genes encoding the bacterial myrosinase enzymes have been identified in |FRAME: TAX-818|, although their exact function is still not completely known |CITS: [32084341]|.</Description>
